The  utility  resolves host and service names to socket addresses
as if with the routine and formats them to standard output.   The
output  is a sequence of lines of space‐separated fields: For the
and address families, the af‐specific data are  the  IP/IPv6  ad‐
dress and port number.  Although the utility may query the DNS to
give  answers, depending on the system’s configuration, it is not
intended to be a general‐purpose utility to query  the  DNS;  use
the  utility for that.  The following options are available: Look
up a canonical name as if with the flag to and print  it  on  the
first  line before the socket addresses.  Specify an address fam‐
ily.  Address families are named like the constants  for  address
family numbers in the header file but without the prefix and low‐
ercase.   For  example, corresponds with Treat the service as nu‐
meric and do not attempt service name resolution, as if with  the
flag  to  Treat  the hostname as a numeric address and do not at‐
tempt name resolution, as if with the flag to Return  socket  ad‐
dresses  intended for use with as if with the flag to By default,
the socket addresses are intended for use with or Specify a  pro‐
tocol.   Protocols may be numeric, or symbolic as listed in Spec‐
ify a service to look up.  Services may be  symbolic  or  numeric
with  an  optional  protocol suffix as listed in If no service is
specified, a hostname must be specified.  Specify a socket  type.
